WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.662
Let's compare a regular Python list with a NumPy array.

00:00:03.662 --> 00:00:07.877
A Python list is a flexible, general-purpose container.

00:00:07.877 --> 00:00:13.247
It can store many kinds of objects, and the plus operator usually means concatenation.

00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:20.962
So if we add a list containing ten, twenty, and thirty to itself, Python creates a longer list.

00:00:20.962 --> 00:00:26.337
A NumPy array behaves differently because it is designed for numerical computing.

00:00:26.337 --> 00:00:29.007
Click Run and compare the outputs.

00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:34.762
When we add the NumPy array to itself, NumPy performs element-by-element addition.

00:00:34.762 --> 00:00:42.397
Ten plus ten becomes twenty, twenty plus twenty becomes forty, and thirty plus thirty becomes sixty.

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:45.392
The multiplication example is also important.

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:50.172
Multiplying the array by two doubles each individual value.

00:00:50.172 --> 00:00:54.092
This is our first example of array-oriented thinking.

00:00:54.092 --> 00:01:01.412
The expression describes what should happen to the entire collection rather than manually looping through each

00:01:01.412 --> 00:01:01.742
value.

00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:08.535
That difference becomes extremely useful when datasets contain thousands or millions of numeric observations.
